        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "b8f881ba-fe6c-5a65-b8ff-37fc3abb3c29",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Stefan Edberg, Édouard Roger-Vasselin, Brad Gilbert, Julien Benneteau, Gilles Müller\n- Events: 1984 unofficial Olympic tournament, 1990 Thriftway ATP Championships – Singles, 2011 Trophée des Alpilles – Doubles, 2014 Roland Garros\n- Topics: tennis achievements, rankings, serve-and-volley style\n\nFacts:\n- Stefan Edberg won the unofficial Olympic tournament in 1984.\n- Stefan Edberg won six Grand Slam singles titles and three Grand Slam men's doubles titles between 1985 and 1996.\n- Stefan Edberg was part of the Swedish Davis Cup-winning team four times.\n- Stefan Edberg won the Masters Grand Prix, four Masters Series titles, and four Championship Series titles.\n- Stefan Edberg was ranked in the singles top 10 for ten consecutive years and in the top 5 for nine years.\n- Stefan Edberg, born on 19 January 1966, is a Swedish former world No. 1 professional tennis player in both singles and doubles.\n- Stefan Edberg was a major proponent of the serve-and-volley style and is considered one of the greatest players of his era.\n- Stefan Edberg defeated defending champion Brad Gilbert 6–1, 6–1 in the final of the 1990 Thriftway ATP Championships – Singles.\n- In 2011, Gilles Müller and Édouard Roger-Vasselin were the defending champions in doubles at the Trophée des Alpilles, but Müller did not participate.\n- In 2014, Édouard Roger-Vasselin won the men's doubles title at Roland Garros with Julien Benneteau.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "368826c3-8948-503e-87ef-8ae12b40af06",
        "text": "Page: Stefan Edberg. Sentence: Stefan Bengt Edberg (] ; born 19 January 1966) is a Swedish former world no. 1 professional tennis player (in both singles and doubles).\nPage: Stefan Edberg. Sentence: A major proponent of the serve-and-volley style of tennis, he won six Grand Slam singles titles and three Grand Slam men's doubles titles between 1985 and 1996.\nPage: Stefan Edberg. Sentence: He also won the Masters Grand Prix and was a part of the Swedish Davis Cup-winning-team four times.\nPage: Stefan Edberg. Sentence: In addition he won four Masters Series titles, four Championship Series titles and the unofficial Olympic tournament 1984, was ranked in the singles top 10 for ten successive years, 9 years in the top 5, and is considered one of the greatest players of his era.\nPage: Édouard Roger-Vasselin. Sentence: He won the men's doubles title at Roland Garros in 2014, partnering Julien Benneteau.\nPage: 1990 Thriftway ATP Championships – Singles. Sentence: Brad Gilbert was the defending champion but Stefan Edberg defeated him 6–1, 6–1 in the final.\nPage: 2011 Trophée des Alpilles – Doubles. Sentence: Gilles Müller and Édouard Roger-Vasselin were the defending champions but Müller decided not to participate.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
